{
  "answer_text": "Anticipate transient network/DNS/TLS failures, rate-limiting, partial or stale responses, inconsistent registry replicas, and full registry outages that can make the registry or its UI hang on \"Loading servers...\" or present a \"Retry\" option. Implement resilient strategies such as exponential backoff with jitter and a capped retry budget, circuit-breakers to fail fast, local caching of validated registry entries/manifests with TTLs and integrity checks for degraded/read-only operation, and configurable fallback or operator-pinned endpoints for critical flows. Also provide conservative fail-closed behavior for sensitive operations, clear diagnostics/metrics/alerts (discovery latency, errors, cache-hits), and audit/manual recovery workflows to enable operator intervention during prolonged outages.",
  "source_quote": "Loading servers...\n\nRetry"
}

{
  "answer_text": "Reusing a request ID within the same MCP session breaks request–response correlation so responses can no longer be unambiguously matched to the original request, leading to misattributed results or races between concurrent operations. The spec requires IDs to be a non-null string or integer and not previously used by the same requestor in the same session, so implementations should generate collision-resistant IDs (for example monotonically increasing integers or UUIDs scoped to the session), track IDs for the lifetime of the session, and validate each incoming ID. On detecting a reused ID, an implementation should treat it as a protocol violation (reject the request, return an error, and/or close the session per policy) and surface logs/telemetry for diagnosis.",
  "source_quote": "Requests MUST include a string or integer ID.\n* Unlike base JSON-RPC, the ID MUST NOT be `null`.\n* The request ID MUST NOT have been previously used by the requestor within the same\n  session."
}

{
  "answer_text": "MCP requires that implementations MAY support sending JSON-RPC batches but MUST support receiving them. Batches are sent as JSON arrays containing multiple requests and notifications, so implementations—especially servers—must be prepared to accept and handle array-encoded messages with multiple requests/notifications even if they themselves never send batches. In practice this means servers must be able to receive and process incoming JSON-RPC arrays according to the JSON-RPC batch mechanism.",
  "source_quote": "JSON-RPC also defines a means to\n[batch multiple requests and notifications](https://www.jsonrpc.org/specification#batch),\nby sending them in an array. MCP implementations MAY support sending JSON-RPC\nbatches, but MUST support receiving JSON-RPC batches."
}

